#
# TODO - cmd
#
# Working notes: what /bin still carries without a source.
#
# /bin has 119 entries and, as before, two build signatures.  A hundred
# and four begin "99 97" and are ccc's, built and installed by this
# tree.  The five Whitesmith binaries the distribution floppies seeded
# that had no source - edit, cpp, newuser, print and td - now have one.
# Twenty more - the Whitesmith
# toolchain and ptc, and the dev tools, spooler and unknowns - were
# moved to /old and are no longer in /bin.
# The distribution left 59, so 33 of them have a source here now.
#
# (One of the 119 is not a command: b1, a ccc-built test program that
# prints "1", left in /bin by a session on 09-28.  Its source is still
# in /tmp/b1.c.  It is counted here because it is in the directory, and
# it wants removing from both.)
#
# Nothing is still in /bin as the distribution's binary.  The four that
# were, in order of how little was missing:
#
#	edit - a line editor of Whitesmith's own, and NOT cmd/vi.  cmd/vi
#	   is STEVIE, a screen editor, and the edit.c in it is one of
#	   STEVIE's modules rather than this program.  It is now cmd/edit,
#	   reconstructed from its Ratfor source (.r files).
#	newuser, print and td - have a source now: cmd/newuser, cmd/print
#	   and cmd/td, each disassembled off the second distribution disk
#	   and read back into C.  All three build with the cross compiler,
#	   have been run in usersim, and are now installed - newuser and
#	   td set-user-id root, as the originals were - so /bin is this
#	   tree's for all three.
#
# The other twenty - the Whitesmith toolchain (as cc link lib obj hex
# lord anat cp1 cp2), ptc, cxr, ddt, rp, pilot, clean, down, and the
# lpr spooler - were moved to /old on 2026-10-01.
#
# far was on this list and is not any more - it was rewritten, and it
# is in both build lists now, so /bin/far is this tree's.
#
# cpp was on this list and is not any more - cmd/cpp is Reiser's own
# v7 preprocessor (usr/src/cmd/cpp), the program attic/hitech/cpp
# forks, and /bin/cpp is this tree's.
#
# cptree was on this list and is not any more - it is cp under a second
# name (cp.c forces -r when called as cptree), so /bin/cptree is this
# tree's.
#
# THE WHITESMITH TOOLCHAIN IS MOVED TO /OLD
#
# as cc link lib obj hex lord anat were the compiler chain the
# distribution was built with, and every one of them is superseded by
# something this tree builds: as by asz, cc by ccc, link by ld, lib by
# ar, obj by nm.  Regenerating them from attic/ was never wanted, so
# their /bin copies were history rather than a gap - there is nothing
# to rebuild them for, and no reason to carry their sources forward.
# They are moved to /old, with ptc, until they are deleted.
#
# cp1 and cp2 belong with them and are the easiest of the ten to
# mistake: they are not two halves of an overlay-based cp, which is
# what an earlier note here guessed from the names.  cp2's own strings
# say p2.80.  They are pass 1 and pass 2 of the Whitesmith C compiler -
# the parser and the 8080 code generator - and ccc's c0 and c1 are
# their replacements, built from libexec.  Their sources are in
# attic/whitesmith/p1 and p2.
#
# /BIN/CPP IS NOT pass0
#
# ccc runs five programs out of /libexec, and not one of them is a
# preprocessor:
#
#	pass0	the lexer and normalizer; its source directory is
#		libexec/pass0
#	c0	pass1, the parser
#	c1	pass2, the code generator
#	peep	the peephole, under -O
#	astpp	AST pretty printer - a debugging aid, not in the
#		compile path at all
#
# pass0 emits a .x stream, "compact token format", and that stream is
# its output: it is what c0 reads, not text anything else consumes.  It
# has text modes, -p writing a readable .i beside the .x and -E handing
# the .x to xdump to render as text, but both are debugging aids over
# the lexeme stream, not a preprocessor's macro-expanded source.
#
# The name cpp is inherited from where the code came from and is wrong.
# The program is pass0, /libexec holds it as pass0, ccc's driver builds
# the path with "pass0" in the format string, and the source directory
# is libexec/pass0 now.  What still says cpp is inside the sources
# themselves - cpp.c and cpp.h - and the driver's own variable and
# header comment ("Orchestrates cpp, c0 (parser), and c1 (code
# generator)").  Renaming those is cosmetic and has not been done.
#
# So /bin/cpp is not the compiler's pass0, and now it is not a gap
# either.  cmd/cpp is John Reiser's own v7 preprocessor (usr/src/cmd/cpp),
# the program the attic/hitech/cpp fork derives from, with cpy.y's yacc
# grammar written by hand for the cross build.  It predefines z80,
# matching ccc's pass0, and installs as /bin/cpp.  See cmd/cpp/README.
#
# cxr, rp and ddt are the same kind of thing: Whitesmith's own tools
# (a C cross-referencer, a macro processor, a debugger), and pilot is
# rp's interpreter.  None of them is a utility anyone is missing, and
# all four are moved to /old.
#
# THE 3 THAT HAD NO SOURCE ANYWHERE IN THE REPOSITORY
#
# newuser, print and td had none, and each has one now, read out of its
# binary: see cmd/newuser, cmd/print and cmd/td, whose READMEs say how.
# td is not a tape dump, as an earlier note here had it: it backs up to
# floppy disks as file systems.
#
# WHICH OF THEM MATTER
#
# A full /bin is not the goal.  What has to exist for the system to
# come up and rebuild itself is a shell, the toolchain, make, and the
# file utilities those need - and all of that is in the 104 this tree
# builds, so none of the last four blocked a bootstrap.  stty was
# the nearest
# to it, being what the terminal wants before anything interactive is
# pleasant; it is ported now.  The rest is a userland that can wait.
#
# The same test is worth applying to the cross DIRS list in
# GNUmakefile: a name belongs there when it is needed to bootstrap the
# others, not merely because a source exists for it.  Everything on
# that list has to be buildable before the thing that builds it.
#
# THE TWO LISTS
#
# They had come apart and they agree now: 96 names in DIRS and 96 in
# the guest makefile, all four rules, checked as sets.  What it took:
#
#	fifteen names were in the cross DIRS and not in the guest list -
#	df djformat halt kstat lz4 md mount mwformat owner packsrc ps
#	umount unlz4 xargs and upm.  Fourteen had native makefiles and
#	were simply forgotten; upm had none at all, because its build is
#	not the generic one - upmhead.s is the crt and the link drives ld
#	with -Ttext=0xd500 - so cmd/upm/makefile was written.  All
#	fifteen were then built by the guest's own make, ccc and ld.
#
#	far, mkfs and fsck were in NEITHER list.  far and mkfs were kept
#	out on purpose, because what they write has to be right - a
#	diskette's directory, a file system - and that wanted checking
#	first; both are in now, and so is fsck.
#
# All three of the new ones build in the guest too.
#
# THE PASSWORD COMMANDS, DONE AND WORTH KNOWING ABOUT
#
# login, owner, chgrp, chown, group, passwd and su now read /etc/passwd
# and /etc/group through libc - getpwent, getgrent, crypt and getpass -
# rather than each carrying its own reader.  crypt was login's own code
# and is a library routine now; it is the pre-DES big-multiply hash,
# not DES, and the salt and the stored string are the same thirteen
# characters they always were.
#
# WHAT IS STILL WORTH DOING
#
# /bin/b1 - the stray test program above - wants deleting, along with
# the copy of its source in /tmp.
#
# vim: tabstop=4 shiftwidth=4 noexpandtab:
